## Executive Summary

This report identifies a critical CSP trust erosion vulnerability where {{.TotalAvailableDomains}} available domain{{if gt .TotalAvailableDomains 1}}s{{end}} ({{range $i, $d := .AvailableDomains}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}{{$d.Domain}}{{end}}) {{if eq .TotalAvailableDomains 1}}is{{else}}are{{end}} whitelisted in Content Security Policy script-src directives but can be registered by attackers for $10-25. The scale of impact is significant: {{range $i, $d := .AvailableDomains}}{{if gt $i 0}}{{if eq $i (sub $.TotalAvailableDomains 1)}} and {{else}}, {{end}}{{end}}{{$d.Domain}} affects {{$d.AffectedCount}} application{{if gt $d.AffectedCount 1}}s{{end}}{{end}}. PublicWWW research confirmed these domains are actively referenced across {{.TotalPublicWWWSites}} unique websites{{if .HighestImpactDomain}}, with {{.HighestImpactDomain}} appearing on {{.HighestImpactCount}} sites including major organizations{{end}}. If an attacker registers any of these domains, they gain JavaScript execution privileges on all affected applications, enabling credential theft, session hijacking, keylogging, and persistent backdoor installation. The attack cost is minimal ($10-15 for most domains) while the blast radius spans potentially thousands of users across enterprise and government organizations.

